problems.problem:
Two countries $A$ and $B$ organize a conference, and they send an equal number of participants. Some of the participants have known each other from a previous conference. Prove that one can choose a nonempty subset $C$ of the participants from $A$ such that one of the following holds:

• each participant from $B$ knows an even number of people in $C$;

• each participant from $B$ knows an odd number of people in $C$.

Prove that any finite set $H$ of lattice points in the plane has a subset $K$ with the following properties:

• any vertical or horizontal line in the plane cuts $K$ in at most $2$ points;

• any point of $H\setminus K$ is contained in a segment with endpoints in $K$.

Prove that for any finite set $A$ of positive integers there exists a subset $B$ of $A$ satisfying the following conditions:

• if $b_1,b_2\in B$ are distinct, then neither $b_1$ and $b_2$ nor $b_1+1$ and $b_2+1$ are multiples of each other;

• for any $a\in A$ we can find $b\in B$ such that $a$ divides $b$ or $b+1$ divides $a+1$.
